<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Methymna</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 129 BCE (approx.) · capital Methymna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Second city of Lesbos, rival of Mytilene, birthplace of the legendary singer Arion and a loyal ally of Athens.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Methymna was founded by Aeolian settlers on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> c. 1000 BCE, on a headland facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troad">Troad</a>. It grew into the second city of the island and a long-standing rival of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mytilene">Mytilene</a> for control of Lesbos.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Methymna was governed mostly by aristocracies and oligarchies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> tells how the singer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arion-of-methymna">Arion of Methymna</a> was saved by a dolphin. In 428 BCE it refused to join Mytilene's revolt and remained an autonomous ship-providing ally in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a>, later joining the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-athenian-league">Second Athenian League</a>. The city was famed for its wine.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Methymna came under Hellenistic kings and in the 2nd century BCE became an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. It was included in the Roman province of Asia from 129 BCE. The town survives as modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molyvos">Mithymna</a>, also called Molyvos.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/methymna.jpg" alt="Methymna" width="320" height="186"><figcaption>Image: Photograph by Mike Peel (www.mikepeel.net). · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Molyvos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 BCE – 129 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Methymna</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aeolic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mithymna</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-564&amp;focus=methymna" aria-label="Methymna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5327 767 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Methymna lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5647 1023L5591 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5647 1023h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5647 1023h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-564&amp;focus=methymna">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

